 all amount of toxins do exist in our  food and are even produced by our bodies  but our bodies have mechanisms to remove  them like the liver and the kidneys the  proponents of detoxes will incorrectly  tell you that under normal circumstances  there's tons of extra harmful muck  building up getting stuck to the walls  of your digestive tract for example and  that you need to flush it all out every  so often this gunk can make you feel  tired anxious depressed bloated whatever  else the debunked detox has come in many  forms you can buy adhesive patches as  cleansing detox footpads which you wear  while you sleep and when you take them  off in the morning you can just see all  the toxins that came out they're  actually just designed to change color  when they contact your natural foot  moisture another popular one is to  eliminate toxins through sweating  profusely the idea being that you can  pump impurities out of your body by  causing perspiration in high-temperature  environments people will do it by going  to the sauna or hot yoga sweating out  toxins is a myth human sweat to lower  their body temperature not to evacuate  harmful substances that's what our  kidneys and liver do sweat is mostly  water salt and potassium  another common cleanse is the juice diet  when you're either buying or making  juices from raw fruits or vegetables  sometimes these juices are all you eat  for the duration of the cleanse if  you're eating or drinking mostly fruit  you miss iron zinc vitamin D calcium  omega-3 fatty acids this can lead to  anemia tiredness and a depleted immune  system too much fruit can cause high  blood sugar it can cause heartburn it  can cause acid reflux and other  conditions many cleanses also involve  eating just excessive amounts of  cruciferous vegetables like broccoli  Brussels sprouts kale or cabbage these  foods are really great for your health  in reasonable quantities not if it's all  you eat vegetables like these contain  thiocyanate switch can affect your  body's ability to absorb iodine which  can lead to hypothyroidism Const  patient dry skin and other issues eating  only fruits and vegetables can also mean  you're missing protein which your immune  system needs to regenerate muscle to  have healthy hair and healthy  fingernails protein is an essential  building block of all kinds of tissue in  your body many diets that consist  exclusively of fruits and sometimes  vegetables are low in calories so people  can use them to lose weight which works  your body responds to the lack of  calories you'll lose a lot of glycogen  you'll lose a lot of water weight but  ultra low calorie crash diets yield what  are often superficial results and  they're not sustainable and as soon as  you start eating normally again the body  will return to its previous state often  you're not getting the nutrients you  need especially if you're eating or  drinking one type of food which is  common in these cleanses it might not  hurt you because you just don't keep it  up for too long although it actually can  harm your body it definitely doesn't  give you any long-term tools for what  you're trying to accomplish whether it's  weight loss or overall health and it  definitely doesn't remove the so-called  toxins from your system another popular  way to remove toxins from your body is  by flushing your bowels either by  ingesting laxatives or through colonics  many people falsely believe that toxins  are left behind and your intestines or  colon when you digest food and that it  needs to be cleared but your bowels  don't work the way that someone pushing  detox makes you think healthy bodies  absorb what they should from food and  the rest comes out it doesn't get stuck  during the process barring an acute  medical issue if you're healthy your  bowels are clear enough from your body's  normal functions and there's no  scientific evidence that colonics are  good for your health or actually flush  anything harmful out and colonics can  also be dangerous the  Gastroenterological community says it's  generally a bad idea  to force flush a healthy person's colon  no matter how you do it and it can cause  unpleasant side effects the hydration  nausea vomiting cramping bloating and  these are only some of  things that : cleansing has been proven  to cause in the US the FDA regulates the  production of the equipment that's used  for this kind of thing but not the use  and that's sort of the issue with a lot  of this stuff drinks and supplements  that brand themselves as detox products  usually have to abide by safety  standards during the manufacturing  process but the FDA doesn't require  these companies to scientifically  substantiate their claims about the  effects of their products because they  aren't actually classified as  medications in 2009 the nonprofit cents  for science contacted 15 companies which  manufacture detox products and they  asked for evidence of their claims the  products included pills smoothies  shampoos none of the 15 companies came  up with evidence and they couldn't even  specify which toxins their product  actually removes from people's bodies  yet this is a massive industry and it's  going to remain massive as long as  people willingly waste their money on  this stuff but why do people do it we  also sometimes hear about air and water  pollution  those are real and we feel like our  bodies are under assault from these  things and in some situations we might  be like for example the scientifically  proven connections between air pollution  and asthma that's real but those  problems aren't going to be solved by  pseudoscience detoxes we know a big part  of this is the placebo effect you just  paid money for something you were told  it was going to work you're expecting  that it will work and the extreme nature  itself with many so-called treatments is  part of the appeal it really feels like  you're doing something major it feels  sort of like okay I'm consuming nothing  but liquid kale or I'm shooting gallons  of water into my colon it's unpleasant  it must be doing something and people do  want to have more control over their  bodies  then we often actually do for example  even if the symptoms someone is trying  to treat with the detox is real someone  might turn to detoxes because there  might not be a sufficient solution to  the problem or using a detox might seem  like a faster or easier solution  to the problem then what doctors might  recommend like improving your diet over  the long term or developing an exercise  program getting better sleep on a  consistent basis the fear of taking  pharmaceutical drugs can also be a  factor many people irrationally  disbelieve in the safety or efficacy of  evidence-based pharmacology or other  medical treatments and instead they turn  to alternative medicine like detoxes but  when it comes to detox is like these  we're talking about ineffective  treatments for problems that are  unspecified or even non-existent in some  cases many people are trying to bounce  back after doing something they consider  unhealthy like binge drinking gaining a  bunch of weight or eating poorly but  there is no substitute for common-sense  solutions to improve your well-being  like eating a balanced diet exercising  doing what your doctor tells you the  reason your doctor won't tell you to go  on a crash Cleanse diet where you only  eat lemon juice and kale for 14 days or  tell you to go spend a weekend at a  sweat lodge to rid yourself of toxins is  because it doesn't work and it would be  immoral for them to recommend it if you  want to learn more about actual  science-based nutrition and physiology  check out class central you can check  out the course they offer from Yale  University called the science of  well-being or the course from Johns  Hopkins University called chemicals and  health click the link in the description  to start searching for top-rated College  lectures from world-renowned  universities